1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:03,960
Imagine driving a car that obeys you three seconds late.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:05,722
That is every day on Mars.

3
00:00:05,762 --> 00:00:07,600
So who do you trust to drive?

4
00:00:08,533 --> 00:00:09,755
We built that day.

5
00:00:09,795 --> 00:00:13,468
One rover, one day of sunlight, rocks to collect, and one rule:

6
00:00:13,508 --> 00:00:15,608
be home before the battery dies.

7
00:00:16,958 --> 00:00:20,468
Then we handed the keys to nine AI models from three labs.

8
00:00:20,508 --> 00:00:22,533
Surely one of them makes it home?

9
00:00:23,541 --> 00:00:24,951
Mars doesn't play fair.

10
00:00:24,991 --> 00:00:28,189
A fast one reaches for a rock and misses, by 3 centimetres.

11
00:00:28,229 --> 00:00:29,138
No points.

12
00:00:29,178 --> 00:00:31,066
9 tries, 1 rock.

13
00:00:32,191 --> 00:00:34,501
A bigger one drives onto a slope too steep.

14
00:00:34,541 --> 00:00:36,864
The rover freezes and forgets its orders.

15
00:00:36,904 --> 00:00:38,266
30 times.

16
00:00:39,491 --> 00:00:43,551
And a flagship: 13 metres from home, 21 samples on board.

17
00:00:43,591 --> 00:00:44,941
It will not make it.

18
00:00:46,541 --> 00:00:47,301
Nothing broke.

19
00:00:47,341 --> 00:00:48,641
So what went wrong?

20
00:00:49,666 --> 00:00:54,001
Its own report says why: the planner counted time, never the battery.

21
00:00:54,041 --> 00:00:57,341
And it played all three maps without checking one result.

22
00:00:58,391 --> 00:00:58,951
The sun.

23
00:00:58,991 --> 00:01:00,276
It's the only fuel.

24
00:01:00,316 --> 00:01:03,266
This rover drove until its battery was flat.

25
00:01:05,474 --> 00:01:08,747
Our driver saw its battery fall, stopped, and waited for light.

26
00:01:08,787 --> 00:01:10,474
So how did all nine do?

27
00:01:11,624 --> 00:01:14,334
On three maps in a row, none beat our driver.

28
00:01:14,374 --> 00:01:16,146
The best run came within a hair.

29
00:01:16,186 --> 00:01:19,621
Ours brought home 48%, and never lost a rover.

30
00:01:19,661 --> 00:01:20,899
So what is it?

31
00:01:22,282 --> 00:01:23,117
Not an AI.

32
00:01:23,157 --> 00:01:26,955
A planner: plain code that works out the whole day before the wheels turn.

33
00:01:26,995 --> 00:01:28,257
Lucky maps?

34
00:01:29,682 --> 00:01:34,517
So we replayed every model's best program on 300 fresh worlds, on Modal.

35
00:01:34,557 --> 00:01:37,317
The planner won 95 times in 100.

36
00:01:37,357 --> 00:01:39,332
Can a machine learn it instead?

37
00:01:40,457 --> 00:01:42,542
We trained a small one from scratch:

38
00:01:42,582 --> 00:01:47,130
from 8% to 36% on unseen worlds, still short of the planner.

39
00:01:47,170 --> 00:01:51,682
Even a model trained to pick the planner's strategy gained less than half a point.

40
00:01:53,174 --> 00:01:54,484
AI can write the code.

41
00:01:54,524 --> 00:01:55,959
It can't yet plan the day.

42
00:01:55,999 --> 00:01:57,749
Can your agent make it home?

